    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin.jpg" alt="Vladimir Lenin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lenin_in_1920_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown, presumably official, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Soviet Union grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution of 1917</a>. In October 1917 the Bolshevik party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> and created what became the Russian Socialist Federative Soviet Republic. In the Civil War of 1918–1921 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, organised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a>, defeated the White armies and foreign intervention forces, and Soviet republics were established or restored by force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasus</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the Baltic states secured their independence. To regulate relations between these nominally sovereign republics, the Bolshevik leaders debated their form: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, People's Commissar for Nationalities, proposed absorbing them into the Russian republic as autonomous units, while Lenin insisted on a federation of formally equal republics. Lenin's view prevailed. On 30 December 1922 the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-soviets-of-the-soviet-union">All-Union Congress of Soviets</a> approved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-on-the-creation-of-the-ussr">Treaty on the Creation of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics</a>, signed by the Russian SFSR, the Ukrainian SSR, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byelorussian-ssr">Byelorussian SSR</a> and the Transcaucasian SFSR. The first constitution, adopted in January 1924, gave each republic the right to secede, while real power lay with the centralised Communist Party. Lenin, already incapacitated by strokes, died in January 1924, opening a struggle for the succession that Stalin won by the end of the decade.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/magnitogorsk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/magnitogorsk.jpg" alt="Magnitogorsk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/magnitogorsk">Magnitogorsk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magnitogorskiy_Gos_Tekh_Universitet.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hellmaster_ru, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Soviet economy was organised on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxist-Leninist</a> principles. After the New Economic Policy of the 1920s, which allowed small private trade and peasant farming, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a> launched the First Five-Year Plan in 1928 and the forced collectivisation of agriculture. Industrial output grew rapidly, with new cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnitogorsk">Magnitogorsk</a>, but collectivisation destroyed much of the peasant economy and caused the famine of 1932–1933, which killed several million people, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, where it is known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a>, and in Kazakhstan. Planning by Gosplan directed resources to heavy industry and defence, while consumer goods and housing remained scarce. Millions passed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> labour camps. Mass literacy campaigns, free education and health care, rapid urbanisation and the promotion of women into the workforce transformed society; by 1989 the population had reached about 287 million, two-thirds of it urban. Religion was repressed, with churches, mosques and synagogues closed, though tolerated in a controlled form after 1943. From the 1960s growth slowed, agriculture depended on grain imports, and the economy relied increasingly on exports of oil and gas. Shortages, a parallel black market and falling productivity marked the era <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> later called stagnation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kursk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kursk.jpg" alt="Battle of Kursk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kursk">Battle of Kursk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Kursk_(map).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, founded in 1918, became one of the largest military forces in the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a>'s purges of 1937–1938 removed much of its senior command, including Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-tukhachevsky">Mikhail Tukhachevsky</a>, weakening it on the eve of war. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> of August 1939 the Soviet Union invaded eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, fought the costly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and annexed the Baltic states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>. The German invasion of 22 June 1941, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a>, inflicted catastrophic losses, but the Red Army halted the Germans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in December 1941, destroyed an entire German army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad">Stalingrad</a> in 1942–1943, won the tank battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kursk">Kursk</a> in July 1943 and smashed Army Group Centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-bagration">Operation Bagration</a> in 1944, before taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in May 1945. About 27 million Soviet citizens died in the war. The Soviet Union then defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in August 1945, tested an atomic bomb in 1949, formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> in 1955 and built a vast nuclear arsenal and ocean-going navy, reaching rough strategic parity with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> by the early 1970s. Soviet troops crushed uprisings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1956 and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1968. The war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 1979 to 1989 became a costly failure.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin.jpg" alt="Joseph Stalin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StalinCropped1943.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franklin D. Roosevelt Library Public Domain Photographs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Soviet Union was formally a federation of union republics, fifteen from 1956, with autonomous republics and regions for smaller nationalities, and a parliament, the Supreme Soviet, elected without real choice. Real power lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> and General Secretary directed state, economy and society; the party's leading role was written into the 1977 constitution. Its system of appointments, the nomenklatura, controlled careers at every level. The political police, successively the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogpu">OGPU</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> and from 1954 the KGB, enforced conformity. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a>, who dominated from the late 1920s until his death in March 1953, terror reached its height: in 1937–1938 alone about 680,000 people were executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a> denounced Stalin's crimes in his secret speech of 1956, presided over the release of most political prisoners from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> and attempted reforms, but was removed in 1964. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a>'s long rule until 1982 brought stability, privilege for officials and growing corruption. Nationality policy fluctuated between the promotion of local languages and elites in the 1920s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> of whole peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> in the 1940s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Gorbachev</a>'s reforms after 1985 introduced contested elections and ended the party's constitutional monopoly in 1990.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin.jpg" alt="Yuri Gagarin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin">Yuri Gagarin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yuri_Gagarin_with_awards_(cropped)_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Александр Моклецов / СовИнформБюро, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Soviet Union was the first state built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxist-Leninist</a> ideology and served as a model, patron and rival for communist movements worldwide. Its victory over Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at enormous cost, decided the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and brought Soviet power into the heart of the continent; communist regimes were installed across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, and the division of Europe and Germany defined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. The Soviet Union supported revolutionary and anti-colonial movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and elsewhere, and the rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> shaped international politics for over four decades, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a> of 1962. Its achievements in science and technology were real: it launched the first artificial satellite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sputnik-1">Sputnik 1</a>, in October 1957 and the first man in space, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin">Yuri Gagarin</a>, on 12 April 1961. Soviet culture combined official socialist realism with world-class achievement in music, ballet, film and literature, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergei-prokofiev">Sergei Prokofiev</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitri-shostakovich">Dmitri Shostakovich</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergei-eisenstein">Sergei Eisenstein</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-pasternak">Boris Pasternak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksandr-solzhenitsyn">Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn</a>, whose account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> shaped Western views of the system. Its legacy still shapes the fifteen successor states, their borders, their large Russian-speaking communities and their conflicts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev.jpg" alt="Mikhail Gorbachev" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_850809_General_Secretary_of_the_CPSU_CC_M._Gorbachev_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vladimir Vyatkin / Владимир Вяткин; derivative work by Jbarta, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the early 1980s the Soviet Union faced economic stagnation, falling oil revenues after 1985, the burden of the arms race and the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and an ageing leadership. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>, General Secretary from March 1985, sought renewal through perestroika, or restructuring, glasnost, or openness, and democratisation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernobyl-disaster">Chernobyl</a> nuclear disaster of April 1986 and the revelations of glasnost undermined confidence in the system, while the economy deteriorated. Gorbachev withdrew from Afghanistan and allowed the communist regimes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> to fall in 1989. Elections to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-peoples-deputies-of-the-soviet-union">Congress of People's Deputies</a> in 1989 and to the republics' parliaments in 1990 empowered national movements: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> declared independence in March 1990, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-yeltsin">Boris Yeltsin</a>, elected president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in June 1991, challenged the union centre. Hardliners attempted a coup on 19–21 August 1991, but it collapsed in the face of resistance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and most republics declared independence. On 8 December the leaders of Russia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belovezha-accords">Belovezha Accords</a>, declaring that the Soviet Union had ceased to exist and founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states">Commonwealth of Independent States</a>. Gorbachev resigned on 25 December, and on 26 December 1991 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-of-the-republics">Soviet of the Republics</a> formally dissolved the union, with Russia taking its seat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Soviet Union was the first communist state and one of the two superpowers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>: it industrialised at a terrible human cost, bore the heaviest burden in defeating Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, pioneered space flight and shaped world politics for half a century before dissolving peacefully into fifteen states in 1991.</em></p>

